    <sys:String x:Key="AboutStep3Body">Built-in pairs, each with English: فارسی، العربية، Français، Italiano، Español، Türkçe، 中文، 한국어. Only the selected one is on. The guide below is for adding another language.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="AboutGuideTitle">Make a language file</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="AboutGuideBody" xml:space="preserve">Each language is one JSON file. The Name field is what you see in the list.

The easy path: click Export all, open the Persian file, change the name and the characters, save it under a new name, then click Import.

1. Layout1Chars is the first keyboard, key by key, left to right and row by row.
2. Layout2Chars is the other keyboard for those same physical keys. The two strings must be the same length. Index n in both strings is one key.
3. Shift is optional. If you add Layout1ShiftChars and Layout2ShiftChars, each must match the length of its base string.
4. Import the file from General or Settings. It becomes the active layout.
5. Only one layout is on. F10 converts with that pair.
6. Export all saves every layout, including ones you imported, into one zip.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="AboutExampleTitle">Example: Chinese ↔ English</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="AboutExampleBody" xml:space="preserve">TypeFix maps one character per key. That is how Persian, Arabic, French, Russian, or a character-based Chinese layout works. A Pinyin input method is not a key-by-key layout, so it does not belong in this file.

Suppose four keys should convert like this: q → 你, w → 好, e → 世, r → 界. Then Layout1Chars is qwer and Layout2Chars is 你好世界. Both lengths are 4. Do the same for every key you care about.

The box below is a full starter. Layout2Chars still matches English, so F10 would change nothing until you replace those characters with the ones your other layout types. Save it as a .json file and click Import.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="AboutCopySample">Copy sample</sys:String>
